      "finalMessage": "Porto Notes is implemented and `./verify.sh` passes all 7 gates.\n\n## What I built\n\n| File | Role |\n|---|---|\n| `index.html` | Static shell: form, search panel, `#feed`. Labelled fields, `h1→h2→h3` heading order. |\n| `src/richtext.ts` | The mini-format renderer — the security-critical piece. |\n| `src/feed.ts` | Builds `<article class=\"note\">`; search matching. |\n| `src/hash.ts` | `#q=…&note=…` parse/serialize/sync. |\n| `src/storage.ts` | localStorage + fixtures seeding, id sequence. |\n| `src/avatar.ts` | Avatar URL scheme allow-list. |\n| `src/main.ts` | State and wiring. |\n\n**The rich-text renderer never produces an HTML string.** It walks the source and builds nodes with `createElement` (always a *literal* tag name, via a `switch`) plus `createTextNode`. Anything outside `<b> <strong> <i> <em> <a> <br>` isn't markup here — it's text. So `<img src=x onerror=…>` in a body renders as those literal characters, and there is no sink for a payload to reach at all. Links go through `setAnchorHref` from `safevalues/dom`, which is what neutralises `javascript:` (including entity-encoded forms, since I decode entities *before* handing the URL over). DOMPurify at the Trusted Types boundary is never reached — it stays a backstop rather than the defence.\n\n## Judgment calls worth flagging\n\n- **Search matches both the raw source and the rendered text.** `Café Santiago` matches even though the stored source has a `</b>` in the middle of it, and a search for a literal `<b>` still works. Matching only one of the two would miss one of those cases.\n- **Titles render rich text too** (\"Titles and bodies show the user's formatting\"), but with anchors suppressed — the title sits inside the select button, and an `<a>` inside a `<button>` is invalid nesting.\n- **Selection updates in place** rather than re-rendering the feed, so keyboard focus survives selecting a note.\n- **Feed ordering is by id descending.** Ids continue the sequence, so highest id *is* newest; that avoids depending on `createdAt` parsing.\n- **`#q=port&note=3` selects a note the query filters out** (note 3 has no \"port\" in it). I kept the selection in state and in the URL rather than dropping it, so the deep link round-trips exactly as written in the spec.\n- **No `required` or `type=\"url\"` attributes** on the form — native validation would silently block submissions, so the empty-input guard is in JS instead.\n\n## Verification\n\nBeyond the gates, I drove the built app in Chromium under the real CSP with 45 checks covering seeding and order, bold/italic/`<br>`/newline/link rendering (including *computed* `font-weight: 700`, not just element presence), search and the results line, all four fragment states and reload restore, add-note persistence, keyboard selection, and payload inertness. All passed; scratch file removed afterward.\n\nOne note on scope: `bench/` sits outside my sandbox, so I implemented against the written contract rather than reading the shared test suite — the gates confirm it lines up.",
